@subsection The Core of the Evaluator

The evaluation process can be described as the interaction between two
procedures: @code{eval} and @code{apply}.

@subsubheading Eval

@code{Eval} takes as arguments an expression and an environment.  It classifies
the expression and directs its evaluation.  @code{Eval} is structured as a case
analysis of the syntactic type of the expression to be evaluated.  In order to
keep the procedure general, we express the determination of the type of an
expression abstractly, making no commitment to any particular representation
for the various types of expressions.  Each type of expression is one
constructor of the typed view @code{Ast.view}, and the fields of the
constructor are its parts.  This @newterm{abstract syntax} makes it easy to see
how we can change the syntax of the language by using the same evaluator, but
with a different collection of syntax procedures.

@noindent
@b{Primitive expressions}

@itemize @bullet

@item
For self-evaluating expressions, such as numbers and strings, @code{eval}
returns the value that the literal denotes.

@item
@code{Eval} must look up variables in the environment to find their values.

@end itemize

@noindent
@b{Special forms}

@itemize @bullet

@item
The object language has no quotation.  Data are built by tuple, list, record,
and constructor expressions, and @code{eval} evaluates the parts of such an
expression and packages the resulting values.

@item
A @code{let} expression must recursively call @code{eval} to compute the values
to be associated with its names, and then evaluate its body in an environment
extended by those bindings.  An assignment @code{r := e} must recursively call
@code{eval} to compute the new value and store it in the reference cell.  The
environment itself never changes.

@item
An @code{if} expression requires special processing of its parts, so as to
evaluate the consequent if the condition is true, and otherwise to evaluate the
alternative.

@item
A @code{fun} expression must be transformed into an applicable procedure by
packaging together the parameters and body specified by the @code{fun}
expression with the environment of the evaluation.

@item
A sequence @code{e1; e2} requires evaluating its expressions in the order in
which they appear.

@item
A @code{match} expression evaluates its scrutinee once and then tries its cases
in source order.  The body of the first case whose pattern matches is evaluated
in an environment that binds the pattern variables.

@item
The operators @code{&&} and @code{||} evaluate their right operand only when
the left operand does not decide the answer.

@end itemize

@noindent
@b{Combinations}

@itemize @bullet

@item
For a procedure application, @code{eval} must recursively evaluate the operator
part and the operands of the combination.  The resulting procedure and
arguments are passed to @code{apply}, which handles the actual procedure
application.

@end itemize

@noindent
Here is the definition of @code{eval}, from the module @code{Sec_4_1}.  The
operator @code{let*} is @code{Result.bind}, so every step that can fail
passes its error on to the caller:

@example ocaml
let rec eval (strategy : strategy) env (e : Ast.expr)
    : outcome =
  match Ast.view e with
  | Ast.Scalar s -> Ok (scalar_value s)
  | Ast.Var name ->
    (match Env.find env name with
    Some v -> Ok v
    | None -> unbound name)
  | Ast.Let (is_rec, bindings, body) ->
    eval_let strategy env is_rec bindings body
  | Ast.Fun (parameters, body) ->
    Ok (Value.closure ~name:None ~parameters ~body ~env)
  | Ast.Apply (fn, args) ->
    let* fn = strategy env fn in
    let* args = eval_list strategy env args in
    apply strategy fn args
  | Ast.If (condition, consequent, alternative) ->
    let* condition = strategy env condition in
    let* b = as_bool "if" condition in
    if b then strategy env consequent
    else strategy env alternative
  | Ast.Match (scrutinee, cases) ->
    let* v = strategy env scrutinee in
    eval_cases strategy env v cases
  | Ast.Tuple parts ->
    let* parts = eval_list strategy env parts in
    Ok (Value.tuple parts)
  | Ast.Construct (name, fields) ->
    let* fields = eval_list strategy env fields in
    Ok (Value.construct name fields)
  | Ast.Record fields ->
    let* fields = eval_fields strategy env fields in
    Ok (Value.record fields)
  | Ast.Field (record, name) ->
    let* record = strategy env record in
    (match Value.view record with
     | Value.Record fields ->
       (match List.assoc_opt name fields with
        | Some v -> Ok v
        | None ->
          type_error ("record field " ^ name ^ " is absent"))
     | _ -> type_error "field access target is not a record")
  | Ast.Sequence (first, second) ->
    let* _ = strategy env first in
    strategy env second
  | Ast.And (left, right) ->
    let* left = strategy env left in
    let* b = as_bool "&&" left in
    if not b then Ok (Value.bool false) else strategy env right
  | Ast.Or (left, right) ->
    let* left = strategy env left in
    let* b = as_bool "||" left in
    if b then Ok (Value.bool true) else strategy env right
  | Ast.Arith (op, left, right) ->
    let* left = strategy env left in
    let* right = strategy env right in
    arithmetic op left right
  | Ast.Compare (op, left, right) ->
    let* left = strategy env left in
    let* right = strategy env right in
    comparison op left right
  | Ast.Nil -> Ok Value.nil
  | Ast.Cons (head, tail) ->
    let* head = strategy env head in
    let* tail = strategy env tail in
    Ok (Value.cons head tail)
  | Ast.Concat (left, right) ->
    let* left = strategy env left in
    let* right = strategy env right in
    (match Value.view left, Value.view right with
     | Value.String a, Value.String b ->
       Ok (Value.string (a ^ b))
     | _ -> type_error "^ operands are not strings")
  | Ast.Not operand ->
    let* operand = strategy env operand in
    let* b = as_bool "not" operand in
    Ok (Value.bool (not b))
  | Ast.Neg operand ->
    let* operand = strategy env operand in
    negate operand
  | Ast.Deref reference ->
    let* reference = strategy env reference in
    (match Value.view reference with
     | Value.Ref cell -> Ok !cell
     | _ -> type_error "! target is not a reference")
  | Ast.Assign (reference, rhs) ->
    let* reference = strategy env reference in
    let* value = strategy env rhs in
    (match Value.view reference with
     | Value.Ref cell ->
       cell := value;
       Ok Value.unit
     | _ -> type_error ":= target is not a reference")
  | Ast.Make_ref operand ->
    let* operand = strategy env operand in
    Ok (Value.ref_value operand)
@end example

@noindent
The first argument, @code{strategy}, says how a subexpression is evaluated.
Its type is @code{Value.env -> Ast.expr -> outcome}, where @code{outcome}
abbreviates @code{(Value.t, Eval_error.t) result}.  The direct evaluator of
this section passes @code{eval} itself, closed over its own strategy:

@example ocaml
let rec direct env body = eval direct env body

let run ~emit program =
  run_items direct (Prelude.initial_env ~emit ())
    (Check.items program)
@end example

@noindent
In @ref{4.1.7} the same @code{eval} runs with a different strategy, and the
exercises supply their own clauses through the open form @code{open_eval
~self}, which evaluates every subexpression through @code{self}.

For clarity, @code{eval} has been implemented as a case analysis using
@code{match}.  The disadvantage of this is that our function handles only a few
distinguishable types of expressions, and no new ones can be defined without
editing the definition of @code{eval}.  In most Lisp implementations,
dispatching on the type of an expression is done in a data-directed style.
This allows a user to add new types of expressions that @code{eval} can
distinguish, without modifying the definition of @code{eval} itself.  (See
@ref{Exercise 4.3}.)

@subsubheading Apply

@code{Apply} takes two arguments, a procedure and a list of arguments to which
the procedure should be applied.  @code{Apply} classifies procedures into two
kinds.  It applies a primitive by calling its OCaml function once the
primitive has all the arguments its arity asks for.  It applies a closure by
evaluating the body of the closure.  The environment for the evaluation of the
body is constructed by extending the environment carried by the closure with a
frame that binds the parameter to the argument.  Guest functions are curried,
as in OCaml: a closure of several parameters takes one argument at a time, and
an application with more arguments than the procedure takes applies the result
to the rest.  Here is the definition of @code{apply}:

@example ocaml
and apply strategy fn args =
  match args with
  | [] -> Ok fn
  | arg :: rest ->
    (match Value.view fn with
     | Value.Closure
         @{ parameters = parameter :: parameters; body; env; _ @} ->
       let env = Env.extend [ parameter, arg ] env in
       if parameters = []
       then (
         let* v = strategy env body in
         apply strategy v rest)
       else
         apply strategy
           (Value.closure ~name:None ~parameters ~body ~env)
           rest
     | Value.Closure @{ parameters = []; _ @} -> not_applicable fn
     | Value.Primitive p ->
       apply_primitive strategy p [] (arg :: rest)
     | Value.Partial (p, gathered) ->
       apply_primitive strategy p gathered (arg :: rest)
     | _ -> not_applicable fn)

and apply_primitive strategy p gathered args =
  let missing = p.prim_arity - List.length gathered in
  if List.length args < missing
  then Ok (Value.partial p (gathered @@ args))
  else (
    let rec split n acc = function
      | rest when n = 0 -> List.rev acc, rest
      | x :: rest -> split (n - 1) (x :: acc) rest
      | [] -> List.rev acc, []
    in
    let taken, rest = split missing [] args in
    let* result =
      p.prim_apply (apply strategy) (gathered @@ taken)
    in
    apply strategy result rest)
@end example

@noindent
A primitive receives @code{apply strategy} as its first argument, so that a
primitive such as @code{List.map} can call a guest closure.

@subsubheading Procedure arguments

When @code{eval} processes a procedure application, it uses @code{eval_list} to
produce the list of arguments to which the procedure is to be applied.
@code{Eval_list} takes as an argument the operands of the combination.  It
evaluates each operand and returns a list of the corresponding
values:@footnote{We could have simplified the @code{Apply} clause in
@code{eval} by using @code{List.map} (the operands are already a list) rather
than writing an explicit @code{eval_list} function.  We chose not to use
@code{map} here to emphasize the fact that the evaluator can be implemented
without any use of higher-order procedures (and thus could be written in a
language that doesn't have higher-order procedures), even though the language
that it supports will include higher-order
procedures.}@footnote{These helpers for lists of expressions are not intended
as a data abstraction.  They are introduced as mnemonic names for the basic
list operations to make it easier to understand the explicit-control evaluator
in @ref{5.4}.}

@example ocaml
and eval_list strategy env exprs =
  let rec go acc = function
    | [] -> Ok (List.rev acc)
    | e :: rest ->
      let* v = strategy env e in
      go (v :: acc) rest
  in
  go [] exprs
@end example

@subsubheading Conditionals

The @code{If} clause of @code{eval} evaluates the condition part of an
@code{if} expression in the given environment.  If the result is true, it
evaluates the consequent, otherwise it evaluates the alternative.  The
condition must be a Boolean, and @code{as_bool} makes that test:

@example ocaml
let as_bool what v =
  match Value.view v with
  | Value.Bool b -> Ok b
  | _ -> type_error (what ^ ": condition is not a bool")
@end example

@noindent
The use of @code{as_bool} in @code{eval} highlights the issue of the connection
between an implemented language and an implementation language.  The condition
is evaluated in the language being implemented and thus yields a value in that
language.  The function @code{as_bool} translates that value into a value that
can be tested by the @code{if} in the implementation language: The
metacircular representation of truth might not be the same as that of the
host, OCaml.@footnote{Here the language being implemented is the OCaml subset
and the implementation language is OCaml.  Contemplation of the meaning of
@code{as_bool} here yields expansion of consciousness without the abuse of
substance.}  The type checker admits only Boolean conditions, so the error
branch of @code{as_bool} never runs on an admitted program.

@subsubheading Sequences

The @code{Sequence} clause of @code{eval} evaluates the expressions of
@code{e1; e2} in the order in which they occur.  It evaluates @code{e1},
discards its value, and returns the value of @code{e2}.  A longer sequence
@code{e1; e2; e3} is the sequence @code{e1; (e2; e3)}, so one clause handles
sequences of any length.

@subsubheading Bindings and assignments

The following procedure handles @code{let} expressions.  A parallel group
evaluates its right-hand sides in the enclosing environment and then extends
that environment by one frame.  A recursive group first binds each name to an
empty cell, so that the right-hand sides can refer to the whole group, and
fills the cells as the right-hand sides produce their values.

@example ocaml
and eval_let strategy env is_rec bindings body =
  if is_rec
  then (
    let names =
      List.filter_map (fun (b : Ast.binding) -> b.name) bindings
    in
    let env, cells = Env.extend_recursive names env in
    let* () = eval_recursive_bindings strategy env cells bindings in
    strategy env body)
  else (
    let* values =
      eval_list strategy env
        (List.map (fun (b : Ast.binding) -> b.rhs) bindings)
    in
    let named =
      List.filter_map
        (fun ((b : Ast.binding), v) ->
          Option.map (fun name -> name, v) b.name)
        (List.combine bindings values)
    in
    strategy (Env.extend named env) body)
@end example

@noindent
Top-level declarations are handled in a similar manner by
@code{run_items}, which extends the global environment one declaration at a
time.@footnote{This treatment of bindings ignores a subtle issue in the
handling of internal definitions, although it works correctly in most cases.
We will see what the problem is and how the object language solves it in
@ref{4.1.6}.}

An assignment changes the contents of a reference cell, not a binding.  The
@code{Assign} clause of @code{eval} evaluates the reference and the new value,
stores the value in the cell, and returns the unit value @code{()}, as
OCaml's @code{:=} does.@footnote{The value of an assignment is fixed by the
type system: @code{:=} has type @code{'a ref -> 'a -> unit}, so the only value
it can return is @code{()}.}

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.1}Exercise 4.1:} Notice that we cannot tell from a
call alone which way the metacircular evaluator evaluates its operands.  Native
OCaml leaves the evaluation order of function arguments unspecified, so the
order is not inherited from the host.  In the teaching evaluator,
@code{eval_list} is an explicit recursion this book writes out, and the order
in which it evaluates operands is fixed by construction.  Write a version of
@code{eval_list} that evaluates operands from left to right by construction.
Also write a version of @code{eval_list} that evaluates operands from right to
left.
@end quotation

@node	4.1.2, 4.1.3, 4.1.1, 4.1
@subsection Representing Expressions

The evaluator is reminiscent of the symbolic differentiation program discussed
in @ref{2.3.2}.  Both programs operate on symbolic expressions.  In
both programs, the result of operating on a compound expression is determined
by operating recursively on the pieces of the expression and combining the
results in a way that depends on the type of the expression.  In both programs
we used data abstraction to decouple the general rules of operation from the
details of how expressions are represented.  In the differentiation program
this meant that the same differentiation procedure could deal with algebraic
expressions in prefix form, in infix form, or in some other form.  For the
evaluator, this means that the syntax of the language being evaluated is
determined solely by the typed view that classifies expressions and carries
their pieces.

The object language is the subset of OCaml that the module @code{Check} admits.
@code{Check.check} parses the source text, rejects every construct outside the
subset's grammar, type-checks the whole unit with the OCaml compiler, and only
then produces the abstract syntax.  No evaluator in this chapter reads source
text itself.  Here is the specification of the syntax of our language, as the
constructors of @code{Ast.view}:

@itemize

@item
The self-evaluating items are the literals of the scalar types:

@example ocaml
type scalar =
  | Int of int
  | Float of float
  | Bool of bool
  | String of string
  | Unit
@end example

@item
Variables are represented by their names: @code{Var of string}.  A
qualified prelude name such as @code{List.map} is one variable whose name
contains the dot.

@item
A @code{let} group is @code{Let of bool * binding list * expr}.  The flag is
@code{true} for @code{let rec}.  Each binding has an optional name, which is
@code{None} for the forms @code{let _ = e} and @code{let () = e}:

@example ocaml
type binding =
  @{ name : string option
  ; rhs : expr
  @}
@end example

@noindent
The declaration @code{let f x y = e} is syntactic sugar for
@code{let f = fun x y -> e}, and @code{Check} produces the same view for both.

@item
Functions are @code{Fun of string list * expr}: the parameter names and the
body.  We also provide a constructor for @code{fun} expressions,
@code{Ast.fun_}, which the derived-expression transformations use.

@item
Applications are @code{Apply of expr * expr list}: the operator and the list of
operands.

@item
Conditionals are @code{If of expr * expr * expr}.  An @code{if} must have an
@code{else} part in the subset, so every conditional has a
condition, a consequent, and an alternative.@footnote{OCaml allows @code{if
c then e} without an @code{else} when @code{e} has type @code{unit}.  The
subset leaves that form out, so the evaluator never has to invent a value
for a missing alternative.}  We also provide a constructor for @code{if}
expressions, @code{Ast.if_}.

@item
A @code{match} is @code{Match of expr * (pattern * expr) list}.  The patterns
have a view of their own:

@example ocaml
type pattern_view =
  | PWildcard
  | PVar of string
  | PScalar of scalar
  | PTuple of pattern list
  | PConstruct of string * pattern list
  | PNil
  | PCons of pattern * pattern
@end example

@item
Data are built by @code{Tuple of expr list}, @code{Construct of string *
expr list}, @code{Record of (string * expr) list}, @code{Nil}, and @code{Cons
of expr * expr}.  A list literal @code{[a; b]} is sugar for @code{a :: b ::
[]}.  A record field is read by @code{Field of expr * string}.

@item
@code{Sequence of expr * expr} packages two expressions into one.

@item
The operators are @code{And}, @code{Or}, @code{Not}, @code{Neg},
@code{Concat}, @code{Arith of arith * expr * expr}, and @code{Compare of
comparison * expr * expr}.  The references are @code{Make_ref},
@code{Deref}, and @code{Assign}.

@end itemize

@noindent
The type @code{Ast.expr} is abstract.  @code{Ast.view} gives its shape, and
the smart constructors @code{Ast.var}, @code{Ast.apply}, @code{Ast.let_}, and
their siblings build new nodes for rewrites and tests.

@subsubheading Derived expressions

Some special forms in our language can be defined in terms of expressions
involving other special forms, rather than being implemented directly.  One
example is @code{function}, which can be implemented as a @code{fun} whose
body is a @code{match}.  For example, we can reduce the problem of evaluating
the expression

@example ocaml
function
| [] -> 0
| x :: _ -> x
@end example

@noindent
to the problem of evaluating the following expression, in which the argument
is given a name and matched explicitly:

@example
fun %function-argument ->
  match %function-argument with
  | [] -> 0
  | x :: _ -> x
@end example

@noindent
Implementing the evaluation of @code{function} in this way simplifies the
evaluator because it reduces the number of special forms for which the
evaluation process must be explicitly specified.  The transformation happens
in @code{Check}, when the source is turned into abstract syntax, so the
evaluator never sees a @code{function} at all.  Here is the clause that
performs it:

@example ocaml
| Pfunction_cases (cases, _, _) ->
  let arg = fresh_function_parameter in
  let* cases = lower_cases ctx inner cases in
  Ok (Ast.fun_ ~at (names @@ [arg])
        (Ast.match_ ~at (Ast.var ~at arg) cases))
@end example

@noindent
The new parameter is named @code{%function-argument}.  No identifier of the
object language can contain @code{%}, so the new name cannot capture a name of
the program.

A transformation that works on a whole expression tree needs to rebuild every
node it does not rewrite.  @code{Ast.map_children f e} is @code{e} with
@code{f} applied to each immediate subexpression, and it is the building block
of the derived-expression rewrites in the exercises.

@noindent
Expressions (such as @code{function}) that we choose to implement as syntactic
transformations are called @newterm{derived expressions}.  @code{Let}
expressions can also be derived expressions (see
@ref{Exercise 4.6}).@footnote{Practical Lisp systems provide a mechanism that
allows a user to add new derived expressions and specify their implementation
as syntactic transformations without modifying the evaluator.  Such a
user-defined transformation is called a @newterm{macro}.  Although it is easy
to add an elementary mechanism for defining macros, the resulting language has
subtle name-conflict problems, the problem the name
@code{%function-argument} avoids above.  There has been much research on
mechanisms for macro definition that do not cause these difficulties.  See, for
example, @ref{Kohlbecker 1986}, @ref{Clinger and Rees 1991}, and
@ref{Hanson 1991}.  OCaml's own preprocessor extensions are outside the
subset, so in this edition the transformations are functions over the typed
syntax.}

@quotation
