@node	4.1.3, 4.1.4, 4.1.2, 4.1
@subsection Evaluator Data Structures

In addition to defining the external syntax of expressions, the evaluator
implementation must also define the data structures that the evaluator
manipulates internally, as part of the execution of a program, such as the
representation of procedures and environments and the representation of true
and false.

@subsubheading Testing of predicates

For conditionals, the only true value is the Boolean @code{true} and the only
false value is @code{false}.  The object language has no rule that treats
other values as true or false, and the type checker rejects a condition that is
not a @code{bool}.  The function @code{as_bool} of @ref{4.1.1} is the whole
test.

@subsubheading Representing procedures

A runtime value is a @code{Value.t}, and @code{Value.view} gives its shape.
Procedures come in two kinds.

@itemize @bullet

@item
A primitive procedure is a record that carries its name, its arity, and the
OCaml function that implements it:

@example ocaml
type primitive =
  @{ prim_name : string
  ; prim_arity : int
  ; prim_apply :
      apply_fun -> t list -> (t, Eval_error.t) result
  @}
@end example

@noindent
Its view is @code{Primitive p}.  A primitive applied to fewer arguments than
its arity is the view @code{Partial (p, args)}.

@item
A compound procedure is a closure, constructed from parameters, a body, and an
environment by @code{Value.closure}:

@example ocaml
Value.closure ~name ~parameters ~body ~env
@end example

@noindent
Its view is @code{Closure c}, where @code{c} has the fields that evaluation
and printing need:

@example ocaml
type closure_view =
  @{ name : string option
  ; parameters : string list
  ; body : Ast.expr
  ; env : env
  @}
@end example

@end itemize

@noindent
These mechanisms for handling primitives are further described in
@ref{4.1.4}.

@subsubheading Operations on Environments

The evaluator needs operations for manipulating environments.  As explained in
@ref{3.2}, an environment is a sequence of frames, where each frame is
a table of bindings that associate variables with their corresponding values.
We use the following operations of the module @code{Env} for manipulating
environments:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{Env.find ⟨@var{env}⟩ ⟨@var{name}⟩}

returns @code{Some v}, where @code{v} is the value that is bound to
@code{⟨}@var{name}@code{⟩} in the nearest binding of the environment
@code{⟨}@var{env}@code{⟩}, or @code{None} if the variable is unbound.

@item
@code{Env.extend ⟨@var{bindings}⟩ ⟨@var{env}⟩}

returns a new environment, consisting of a new frame in which each name in the
list of name-value pairs @code{⟨}@var{bindings}@code{⟩} is bound to its value,
where the enclosing environment is the environment
@code{⟨}@var{env}@code{⟩}.

@item
@code{Env.extend_recursive ⟨@var{names}⟩ ⟨@var{env}⟩}

returns a new environment in which each name of @code{⟨}@var{names}@code{⟩}
is bound to a fresh empty cell, together with the list of those cells.

@item
@code{Env.fill ⟨@var{cell}⟩ ⟨@var{value}⟩}

completes the recursive binding @code{⟨}@var{cell}@code{⟩} with the value
@code{⟨}@var{value}@code{⟩}.

@end itemize

@noindent
There is no operation that changes the value of a binding.  In OCaml a binding
is immutable, and mutation lives in reference cells, which are values.  Every
closure that captures the same reference observes the same cell, so the
environment model of @ref{3.2} still describes assignment: the binding holds
the reference, and the reference holds the changing contents.

To implement these operations we represent an environment as one list of
bindings, newest first.  Each binding pairs a name with a cell:

@example ocaml
type env = (string * t option ref) list

let env_extend bindings env =
  List.map (fun (name, v) -> name, ref (Some v)) bindings @@ env

let env_extend_recursive names env =
  let cells = List.map (fun name -> name, ref None) names in
  cells @@ env, List.map snd cells

let env_fill cell v = cell := Some v
@end example

@noindent
The frames are not marked in the list: a frame is the run of bindings that one
call of @code{extend} put in front.  The cells are there only for recursive
groups.  An ordinary binding is created full and is never
changed.@footnote{The bindings are not really a data abstraction in this code:
the cells live behind @code{Env}'s operations, which the evaluator calls to
make and fill them.  The purpose of the @code{Env} operations is to make the
environment-manipulation procedures easy to read.}

@noindent
To look up a variable in an environment, we search the list from the newest
binding.  If we find the desired variable, we return its value.  If no binding
names the variable, @code{Env.find} returns @code{None} and @code{eval}
signals an ``unbound variable'' error.  The type checker has already resolved
every name, so this error never happens on an admitted program.

@example ocaml
let env_find env name =
  match List.assoc_opt name env with
  | None -> None
  | Some cell ->
    (match !cell with
     | Some v -> Some v
     | None -> Some (VIndirect cell))
@end example

@noindent
A cell that is still empty is read while its recursive group is being
evaluated.  The lookup then returns an indirect value that will hold the
cell's contents once the group is complete, which is what a closure
that names itself needs.

@noindent
The method described here is only one of many plausible ways to represent
environments.  Since we used data abstraction to isolate the rest of the
evaluator from the detailed choice of representation, we could change the
environment representation if we wanted to.  (See @ref{Exercise 4.11}.)  In a
production-quality system, the speed of the evaluator's environment
operations, especially that of variable lookup, has a major impact on the
performance of the system.  The representation described here, although
conceptually simple, is not efficient and would not ordinarily be used in a
production system.@footnote{The drawback of this representation (as well as the
variant in @ref{Exercise 4.11}) is that the evaluator may have to search
through many bindings to find the binding for a given variable.  (Such
an approach is referred to as @newterm{deep binding}.)  One way to avoid this
inefficiency is to make use of a strategy called @newterm{lexical addressing},
which will be discussed in @ref{5.5.6}.}

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.11}Exercise 4.11:} Instead of representing an
environment as one list of bindings, we can represent it as a list of frames,
where each frame is a pair of lists: the list of the names the frame binds
and the list of their values, in the same order.  Rewrite the environment
operations to use this alternative representation.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.12}Exercise 4.12:} Over the representation of
@ref{Exercise 4.11}, the procedures that look up a variable, that change the
value of an existing binding, and that add a binding to the first frame can be
expressed in terms of more abstract procedures for traversing the environment
structure.  Define abstractions that capture the common patterns and redefine
the three procedures in terms of these abstractions.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.13}Exercise 4.13:} The object language lets us
create new bindings for variables by means of @code{let}, but provides no way
to get rid of bindings.  Over the frame representation of @ref{Exercise 4.11},
implement for the evaluator a special form @code{make_unbound x} that removes
the binding of @code{x} from the environment in which the @code{make_unbound}
expression is evaluated.  This problem is not completely specified.  For
example, should we remove only the binding in the first frame of the
environment?  Complete the specification and justify any choices you make.
@end quotation

@node	4.1.4, 4.1.5, 4.1.3, 4.1
@subsection Running the Evaluator as a Program

Given the evaluator, we have in our hands a description (expressed in OCaml) of
the process by which the expressions of the object language are evaluated.  One
advantage of expressing the evaluator as a program is that we can run the
program.  This gives us, running within OCaml, a working model of how OCaml
itself evaluates expressions.  This can serve as a framework for experimenting
with evaluation rules, as we shall do later in this chapter.

Our evaluator program reduces expressions ultimately to the application of
primitive procedures.  Therefore, all that we need to run the evaluator is to
create a mechanism that calls on the OCaml runtime to model the
application of primitive procedures.

There must be a binding for each primitive procedure name, so that when
@code{eval} evaluates the operator of an application of a primitive, it will
find an object to pass to @code{apply}.  We thus set up a global environment
that associates a primitive value with each name of the fixed standard-library
surface: the eight unqualified names @code{print_string}, @code{print_endline},
@code{print_int}, @code{print_newline}, @code{string_of_int},
@code{string_of_float}, @code{float_of_int}, and @code{sqrt}, and the admitted
members of @code{Array}, @code{List}, and @code{Hashtbl}.  The Booleans
@code{true} and @code{false} are literals, so they need no binding.  The module
@code{Prelude} builds this environment:

@example ocaml
let initial_env ~emit () =
  let prim name arity apply =
    name, Value.primitive ~name ~arity apply
  in
  let bindings =
    [ prim "print_string" 1 (fun _apply args ->
        one "print_string" args
        >>= (as_string "print_string")
        |> Result.map (fun s ->
          emit s;
          Value.unit))
    @r{…}
    ]
  in
  Env.extend bindings Env.empty
@end example

@noindent
The printing primitives write through @code{emit}, so a caller decides where
the output of the guest program goes.  @code{Sec_4_1} names this environment
@code{the_global_environment}:

@example ocaml
let the_global_environment ?(emit = print_string) () =
  Prelude.initial_env ~emit ()
@end example

@noindent
It does not matter how we represent the primitive procedure objects, so long as
@code{apply} can identify and apply them by their view.  A primitive that takes
a procedure as an argument receives the evaluator's own @code{apply} and calls
the guest procedure through it.  Here is @code{List.map}:@footnote{Any function
written in OCaml can be used as a primitive for the metacircular evaluator.
The name of a primitive installed in the evaluator need not be the same as the
name of its implementation in OCaml; the names are the same here because the
metacircular evaluator implements the OCaml subset itself.  Thus, for example,
we could put an entry named @code{first} for a function that returns the first
element of a list, or an entry named @code{square} for a squaring function, in
the list of @code{initial_env}.  The type checker would then reject every
program that uses them, because it checks a program against the real OCaml
names.}

@example ocaml
prim "List.map" 2 (fun apply args ->
  two "List.map" args
  >>= (fun (f, items) ->
    let* f = as_procedure "List.map" f in
    let* items = as_list "List.map" items in
    let rec map acc = function
      | [] -> Ok (value_of_list (List.rev acc))
      | item :: rest ->
        Result.bind (apply f [ item ])
          (fun v -> map (v :: acc) rest)
    in
    map [] items))
@end example

@noindent
A primitive that skips @code{apply} and calls a host list function directly
receives a value of the object language where the host expects an OCaml
function, and fails on any compound procedure (see @ref{Exercise 4.14}).

To apply a primitive procedure, we simply apply the implementation function to
the arguments, an ordinary OCaml call of the function the record
stores.@footnote{The metacircular evaluator's @code{apply} (@ref{4.1.1}) models
the working of the host's application.  In OCaml the two never share a name,
so defining the metacircular @code{apply} masks nothing.}

@noindent
For convenience in running the metacircular evaluator, we provide a
@newterm{driver} that models the way we run an OCaml program.  It admits a
whole program with @code{Check.check}, runs it with an evaluator, and collects
what the program prints.  If the program stops on a runtime error, the driver
appends the error; if admission fails, the driver reports the stage that
rejected the source.  The object language has no reader loop and no prompt:
the output of a program is exactly the bytes its printing primitives
write.@footnote{Admission rejects the whole program before any part of it runs,
so a program with a type error prints nothing at all.  This is the behavior of
the OCaml compiler, which refuses to produce an executable for such a
program.}

@example ocaml
let transcript ?(experiment = Check.Core) run source =
  match
    Check.check_experiment ~experiment
      ~filename:"program.ml" source
  with
  | Error d -> "rejected: " ^ Check.kind_to_string d.kind
  | Ok program ->
    let out = Buffer.create 64 in
    (match run ~emit:(Buffer.add_string out) program with
     | Ok _ -> Buffer.contents out
     | Error e ->
       Buffer.contents out ^ "error: "
       ^ Eval_error.to_string e)
@end example

@noindent
Now all we need to do to run the evaluator is to give a program to the driver.
Here is a sample, whose output is shown after the arrow:

@example ocaml
Sec_4_1.transcript Sec_4_1.run
  "let rec append x y =
     match x with [] -> y | h :: t -> h :: append t y
   let rec show xs =
     match xs with
     | [] -> \"\"
     | [ x ] -> x
     | x :: rest -> x ^ \" \" ^ show rest
   let () =
     print_endline
       (show (append [ \"a\"; \"b\"; \"c\" ]
                     [ \"d\"; \"e\"; \"f\" ]))"
(* => "a b c d e f\n" *)
@end example

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.14}Exercise 4.14:} Eva Lu Ator and Louis Reasoner
are each experimenting with the metacircular evaluator.  Eva types in the
definition of @code{map}, and runs some test programs that use it.  They work
fine.  Louis, in contrast, has installed OCaml's own @code{List.map} as a
primitive for the metacircular evaluator: his primitive hands the guest
procedure straight to the host function, which expects an OCaml function, not
a value of the object language.  When he tries it with a procedure defined in
the object language, things go terribly wrong.  Explain why Louis's
@code{map} fails even though Eva's works.  The @code{List.map} of
@code{Prelude} also works: what does it do differently with the procedure it
receives?
@end quotation

@quotation
@emph{Exercise 4.14a is added by this edition and extends exercise
4.14; SICP numbers stop at 4.14.} @strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.14a}Exercise 4.14a:} Eva answers Louis by writing her own
@code{sort} in the object language and sorting the same test data
with a compound comparison procedure.  Louis installs the host's
list-sorting procedure as the primitive @code{List.sort} in the same way,
handing it the guest comparison directly.  Sorting with a host comparison
works, but sorting with a compound procedure goes badly wrong.  Explain why
Louis's @code{sort} fails on the compound comparison even though Eva's
works.
@end quotation

@node	4.1.5, 4.1.6, 4.1.4, 4.1
@subsection Data as Programs

In thinking about an OCaml program that evaluates OCaml programs, an analogy
might be helpful.  One operational view of the meaning of a program is that a
program is a description of an abstract (perhaps infinitely large) machine.
For example, consider the familiar program to compute factorials:

@example ocaml
let rec factorial n =
  if n = 1 then 1 else factorial (n - 1) * n

let () = print_int (factorial 5)
@end example

@noindent
Given to the driver of @ref{4.1.4}, this program prints @code{120}.

@noindent
We may regard this program as the description of a machine containing parts
that decrement, multiply, and test for equality, together with a two-position
switch and another factorial machine. (The factorial machine is infinite
because it contains another factorial machine within it.)  @ref{Figure 4.2} is
a flow diagram for the factorial machine, showing how the parts are wired
together.

@float
@anchor{Figure 4.2}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 4.2:} The factorial program, viewed as an abstract machine.

@example
    +-----------------------------------+
    | factorial                   |1    |
    |              |1             V     |
    |              |           +-----+  |
    |              V           | #   |  |
    |           +-----+        |     |  |
6 --------*-----|  =  |------->|   #-+-----> 720
    |     |     +-----+        |  /  |  |
    |     |                    | #   |  |
    |     |                    +-----+  |
    |     |                       ^     |
    |     |                       |     |
    |     |                    +--+--+  |
    |     *------------------->|  *  |  |
    |     |                    +-----+  |
    |     V                       ^     |
    |  +-----+    +-----------+   |     |
    |  |  -  +--->| factorial +---+     |
    |  +-----+    +-----------+         |
    |     ^                             |
    |     |1                            |
    +-----------------------------------+
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap4/Fig4.2a,,143mm,Figure 4.2: The factorial program@comma{} viewed as an abstract machine.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap4/Fig4.2a.std,147mm,,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 4.2:} The factorial program, viewed as an abstract machine.}
@end float

In a similar way, we can regard the evaluator as a very special machine that
takes as input a description of a machine.  Given this input, the evaluator
configures itself to emulate the machine described.  For example, if we feed
our evaluator the definition of @code{factorial}, as shown in @ref{Figure 4.3},
the evaluator will be able to compute factorials.

@float
@anchor{Figure 4.3}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 4.3:} The evaluator emulating a factorial machine.

@example
                   +--------+
            6 ---->|  eval  |----> 720
                   +--------+
                       /
             . . .    /  . . .
       . . .       ../. .      .
     .                           ..
    .   let rec factorial n =      . . .
   .      if n = 1                      . .
    .     then 1                           .
    .     else factorial (n - 1) * n        .
      . .                       . .        .
          . .  . .      . . . .     . . . .
                   . ..
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap4/Fig4.3,,117mm,Figure 4.3: The evaluator emulating a factorial machine.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap4/Fig4.3.std,147mm,,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 4.3:} The evaluator emulating a factorial machine.}
@end float

From this perspective, our evaluator is seen to be a @newterm{universal machine}.  
It mimics other machines when these are described as programs of
the object language.@footnote{The fact that the machines are described in
OCaml is inessential.  If we give our evaluator an OCaml program that behaves
as an evaluator for some other language, say C, the OCaml evaluator will
emulate the C evaluator, which in turn can emulate any machine described as a C
program.  Similarly, writing an OCaml evaluator in C produces a C program that
can execute any OCaml program.  @ref{Exercise 5.51} builds such a program.  The deep idea here is that any evaluator can emulate any
other.  Thus, the notion of ``what can in principle be computed'' (ignoring
practicalities of time and memory required) is independent of the language or
the computer, and instead reflects an underlying notion of
@newterm{computability}.  This was first demonstrated in a clear way by Alan
M. Turing (1912-1954), whose 1936 paper laid the foundations for theoretical
computer science.  In the paper, Turing presented a simple computational
model, now known as a @newterm{Turing machine}, and argued that any
``effective process'' can be formulated as a program for such a machine.  (This
argument is known as the @newterm{Church-Turing thesis}.)  Turing then
implemented a universal machine, i.e., a Turing machine that behaves as an
evaluator for Turing-machine programs.  He used this framework to demonstrate
that there are well-posed problems that cannot be computed by Turing machines
(see @ref{Exercise 4.15}), and so by implication cannot be formulated as
``effective processes.''  Turing went on to make fundamental contributions to
practical computer science as well.  For example, he invented the idea of
structuring programs using general-purpose subroutines.  See @ref{Hodges 1983} for a
biography of Turing.} This is striking. Try to imagine an analogous evaluator
for electrical circuits.  This would be a circuit that takes as input a signal
encoding the plans for some other circuit, such as a filter.  Given this input,
the circuit evaluator would then behave like a filter with the same
description.  Such a universal electrical circuit is almost unimaginably
complex.  It is remarkable that the program evaluator is a rather simple
program.@footnote{Some people find it counterintuitive that an evaluator, which
is implemented by a relatively simple procedure, can emulate programs that are
more complex than the evaluator itself.  The existence of a universal evaluator
machine is a deep and wonderful property of computation.  @newterm{Recursion theory}, 
a branch of mathematical logic, is concerned with logical limits of
computation.  Douglas Hofstadter's beautiful book @cite{G@"odel, Escher, Bach}
explores some of these ideas (@ref{Hofstadter 1979}).}

@noindent
Another striking aspect of the evaluator is that it acts as a bridge between
the data objects that are manipulated by our programming language and the
programming language itself.  Imagine that the evaluator program (implemented
in OCaml) is running, and that a user is typing expressions to the evaluator and
observing the results.  From the perspective of the user, an input expression
such as @code{x * x} is an expression in the programming language, which the
evaluator should execute.  From the perspective of the evaluator, however, the
expression is simply data (in this case, the value @code{Arith (Mul, x, x)} of
the view, whose operands are two occurrences of the variable @code{x}) that is
to be manipulated according to a well-defined set of rules.

That the user's programs are the evaluator's data need not be a source of
confusion.  In fact, it is sometimes convenient to ignore this distinction, and
to give the user the ability to explicitly evaluate a data object as an
expression, by making @code{eval} available for use in programs.  Many Lisp
dialects provide a primitive @code{eval} procedure that takes as arguments an
expression and an environment and evaluates the expression relative to the
environment.  OCaml has no such primitive, and the object language admits none:
a program that wants to evaluate data as a program must contain an evaluator,
as the programs of @ref{Exercise 5.50} do.@footnote{Warning: An @code{eval}
primitive of a Lisp system is not identical to the @code{eval} procedure we
implemented in @ref{4.1.1}, because it uses @emph{actual} environments of the
host rather than the sample environment structures we built in
@ref{4.1.3}.  These actual environments cannot be manipulated by the user as
ordinary lists; they must be accessed via @code{eval} or other special
operations.  Similarly, a host @code{apply} primitive is not identical to the
metacircular @code{apply}, because it uses actual host procedures rather than
the procedure objects we constructed in @ref{4.1.3} and @ref{4.1.4}.}  From
the host side, the teaching evaluator offers the two routes.  Thus,

@example ocaml
Sec_4_1.transcript Sec_4_1.run "let () = print_int (5 * 5)"
@end example

@noindent
and

@example ocaml
let five = Ast.scalar (Ast.Int 5) in
Sec_4_1.eval_expr (Ast.arith Ast.Mul five five)
  (Sec_4_1.the_global_environment ())
@end example

@noindent
will both produce 25.@footnote{The first route admits a whole program from
text with @code{Check.check} and runs it.  The second evaluates an expression
built directly from the smart constructors of @code{Ast}, here in the global
environment.  The smart constructors skip the type checker, so a program built
this way is trusted to be well typed.}

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.15}Exercise 4.15:} Given a one-argument procedure
@code{p} and an object @code{a}, @code{p} is said to ``halt'' on @code{a} if
evaluating the expression @code{p a} returns a value (as opposed to
terminating with an error message or running forever).  Show that it is
impossible to write a procedure @code{halts} that correctly determines whether
@code{p} halts on @code{a} for any procedure @code{p} and object @code{a}.  Use
the following reasoning: If you had such a procedure @code{halts}, you could
implement the following program:

@example
let rec run_forever _ = run_forever ()

let try_ p =
  if halts p p then run_forever () else "halted"
@end example

Now consider evaluating the expression @code{try_ try_} and show that any
possible outcome (either halting or running forever) violates the intended
behavior of @code{halts}.  (The names differ from the original book's:
@code{try} is a keyword and @code{halts?} is not an identifier.  With
@code{halts} of type @code{'a -> 'b -> bool}, the self-application
@code{try_ try_} type-checks, so the argument stands as written.)@footnote{Although we
stipulated that @code{halts}
is given a procedure object, notice that this reasoning still applies even if
@code{halts} can gain access to the procedure's text and its environment.
This is Turing's celebrated @newterm{Halting Theorem}, which gave the first
clear example of a @newterm{non-computable} problem, i.e., a well-posed task
that cannot be carried out as a computational procedure.}
@end quotation

@node	4.1.6, 4.1.7, 4.1.5, 4.1
@subsection Internal Definitions

Our environment model of evaluation and our metacircular evaluator execute the
top-level declarations of a program in sequence, extending the environment one
declaration at a time.  This is convenient for building a program piece by
piece, in which each new procedure uses the procedures declared before it.
However, if we think carefully about the internal definitions used to
implement block structure (introduced in @ref{1.1.8}), we will find that
name-by-name extension of the environment may not be the best way to define
local variables.

Consider a procedure with internal definitions, such as

@example
let f x =
  let rec is_even n =
    if n = 0 then true else is_odd (n - 1)
  and is_odd n =
    if n = 0 then false else is_even (n - 1)
  in
  ⟨@var{rest of body of @code{f}}⟩
@end example

@noindent
Our intention here is that the name @code{is_odd} in the body of the procedure
@code{is_even} should refer to the procedure @code{is_odd} that is defined
after @code{is_even}.  The scope of the name @code{is_odd} is the entire group,
not just the portion of the group starting at the point where the binding for
@code{is_odd} occurs.  Indeed, when we consider that @code{is_odd} is itself
defined in terms of @code{is_even}, so that @code{is_even} and @code{is_odd}
are mutually recursive procedures, we see that the only satisfactory
interpretation of the two definitions is to regard them as if the names
@code{is_even} and @code{is_odd} were being added to the environment
simultaneously.

OCaml makes the programmer state which interpretation is meant.  A sequence of
@code{let ... in} bindings is sequential: each name is visible only in the
expressions that follow it.  A group @code{let rec ... and ...} is
simultaneous: every name of the group is visible in every right-hand side.  If
we write the two procedures above with two plain @code{let}s, the type checker
rejects the program, because the name @code{is_odd} is unbound in the body of
@code{is_even}.  A language whose internal definitions look sequential but are
meant to be simultaneous must choose an evaluation rule instead, and programs
that depend on the choice are ``badly formed.''@footnote{Wanting programs to
not depend on this evaluation mechanism is the reason for the ``management is
not responsible'' remark in @ref{Footnote 28} of @ref{Chapter 1}.  The original book's Lisp dialect insists that internal definitions come first and
do not use each other while the definitions are being evaluated, and so leaves
implementors some choice in the mechanism used to evaluate these definitions.
The choice of one evaluation rule rather than another here may seem like a
small issue, affecting only the interpretation of ``badly formed'' programs.
However, we will see in @ref{5.5.6} that moving to a model of simultaneous
scoping for internal definitions avoids some nasty difficulties that would
otherwise arise in implementing a compiler.}  (For an example of a procedure
in which sequential and simultaneous definition differ, see
@ref{Exercise 4.19}.)

There is a simple way to give the names of a group truly simultaneous scope:
create all the local variables of the group before evaluating any of the
value expressions.  Our evaluator does exactly this.  @code{Env.extend_recursive}
binds every name of the group to an empty cell, the right-hand sides are
evaluated in the extended environment, and @code{Env.fill} completes each cell
in turn (see @code{eval_let} in @ref{4.1.1}).

The same effect can be reached by a syntax transformation on the object
language itself.  Before evaluating a recursive group, we ``scan out'' the
group.  The names are created with a @code{let} that binds each to a
reference holding a special marker, and are then set to their values by
assignment.  For example, the expression

@example
let rec u = ⟨@var{e1}⟩
and v = ⟨@var{e2}⟩ in
⟨@var{e3}⟩
@end example

@noindent
would be transformed into

@example
let u = ref unassigned
and v = ref unassigned in
u := ⟨@var{e1}⟩;
v := ⟨@var{e2}⟩;
⟨@var{e3}⟩
@end example

@noindent
where every use of @code{u} or @code{v} inside @code{⟨}@var{e1}@code{⟩},
@code{⟨}@var{e2}@code{⟩}, and @code{⟨}@var{e3}@code{⟩} is rewritten into
@code{!u} or @code{!v}, and @code{unassigned} is a special marker value that
causes looking up a variable to signal an error if an attempt is made to use
the value of the not-yet-assigned variable.

An alternative strategy for scanning out internal definitions is shown in
@ref{Exercise 4.18}.  Unlike the transformation shown above, this enforces the
restriction that the defined variables' values can be evaluated without using
any of the variables' values.@footnote{The Lisp dialect of the original book
allows for different implementation strategies by specifying that it is up to
the programmer to obey this restriction, not up to the implementation to
enforce it.  Some implementations use the transformation shown above.  Thus,
some programs that don't obey this restriction will in fact run in such
implementations.  OCaml takes the other route: its compiler checks the
restriction statically, and admits a right-hand side of a @code{let rec} only
if it is a function or a data construction whose recursive occurrences lie
beneath a constructor.  A group such as @code{let rec a = b + 1 and b = 2}
is rejected before the program runs.}

@quotation
@strong{In this language: a typed abstract syntax and pattern-matched dispatch.}

The book's evaluator classifies an expression by asking it
predicates; this edition's @code{Ast} hands @code{eval} one
@code{Ast.view} value whose constructor @emph{is} the expression
type, so the dispatch of @ref{4.1.1} is a @code{match} with one arm
per syntactic form, and the compiler checks that no arm is missing.

@example ocaml
match Ast.view e with
| Ast.Scalar s -> Ok (scalar_value s)
| Ast.Var name -> @r{…}
| Ast.Apply (fn, args) -> @r{…}
@r{…}
@end example

The book's mechanism still wins when the set of forms must grow
while the program runs: exercise 4.3's table accepts a handler for a
new type at runtime, where a @code{match} must be edited and
recompiled.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.16}Exercise 4.16:} In this exercise we implement the
method just described for interpreting internal definitions.  We assume that
the evaluator supports @code{let} (see @ref{Exercise 4.6}).

@enumerate a

@item
Change variable lookup (the @code{Var} clause of @code{eval} in @ref{4.1.1})
to signal an error if the value it finds is the @code{unassigned} marker.

@item
Write a procedure @code{scan_out_defines} that takes a procedure body and
returns an equivalent one that has no recursive group, by making the
transformation described above.

@item
Install @code{scan_out_defines} in the interpreter, either where closures
are constructed (the @code{Fun} clause of @code{eval}) or where the body is
taken for evaluation (the @code{Closure} branch of @code{apply}, see
@ref{4.1.1}).  Which place is better?  Why?

@end enumerate

Because the type checker already rejects a group that reads a name too early,
test the error path on syntax built with the constructors of @code{Ast}.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.17}Exercise 4.17:} Draw diagrams of the environment
in effect when evaluating the expression @code{⟨}@var{e3}@code{⟩} in the
example in the text, comparing how this will be structured when the group is
bound directly by @code{Env.extend_recursive} with how it will be structured
if the group is scanned out as described.  Why is there an extra frame in the
transformed program?  Explain why this difference in environment structure can
never make a difference in the behavior of a correct program.  Design a way to
make the interpreter implement the ``simultaneous'' scope rule for internal
definitions without constructing the extra frame.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.18}Exercise 4.18:} Consider an alternative strategy
for scanning out definitions that translates the example in the text to

@example
let u = ref unassigned
and v = ref unassigned in
(let a = ⟨@var{e1}⟩
 and b = ⟨@var{e2}⟩ in
 u := a;
 v := b);
⟨@var{e3}⟩
@end example

Here @code{a} and @code{b} are meant to represent new variable names, created
by the interpreter, that do not appear in the user's program.  Consider the
@code{solve} procedure from @ref{3.5.4}, written with a recursive group:

@example
let solve f y0 dt =
  let rec y = integral (delay dy) y0 dt
  and dy = stream_map f y in
  y
@end example

Will this procedure work if internal definitions are scanned out as shown in
this exercise?  What if they are scanned out as shown in the text?  Explain.
(The OCaml compiler rejects this group, which is why @ref{3.5.4} backpatches a
cell by hand.  Answer for the scanned-out syntax, and check your answer on the
smaller group @code{let rec a _ = 1 and b = a () + 1 in b}, built with the
constructors of @code{Ast}.)
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.19}Exercise 4.19:} Ben Bitdiddle, Alyssa P. Hacker,
and Eva Lu Ator are arguing about the desired result of evaluating the
expression

@example
let a = 1 in
let f x =
  let rec b = a + x
  and a = 5 in
  a + b
in
f 10
@end example

Ben asserts that the result should be obtained using the sequential rule for
definitions: @code{b} is defined to be 11, then @code{a} is defined to be 5,
so the result is 16.  Alyssa objects that mutual recursion requires the
simultaneous scope rule for internal procedure definitions, and that it is
unreasonable to treat procedure names differently from other names.  Thus, she
argues for the mechanism implemented in @ref{Exercise 4.16}.  This would lead
to @code{a} being unassigned at the time that the value for @code{b} is to be
computed.  Hence, in Alyssa's view the procedure should produce an error.  Eva
has a third opinion.  She says that if the definitions of @code{a} and @code{b}
are truly meant to be simultaneous, then the value 5 for @code{a} should be
used in evaluating @code{b}.  Hence, in Eva's view @code{a} should be 5,
@code{b} should be 15, and the result should be 20.  Which (if any) of these
viewpoints do you support?  Can you devise a way to implement internal
definitions so that they behave as Eva prefers?  The OCaml compiler rejects
this program.  Which of the three positions does that choice
take?@footnote{The @abbr{MIT} implementors of the original book's language
support Alyssa on the following grounds: Eva is in principle correct, and the
definitions should be regarded as simultaneous.  But it seems difficult to
implement a general, efficient mechanism that does what Eva requires.  In the
absence of such a mechanism, it is better to generate an error in the
difficult cases of simultaneous definitions (Alyssa's notion) than to produce
an incorrect answer (as Ben would have it).}
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.20}Exercise 4.20:} Because internal definitions look
sequential but are actually simultaneous, some languages avoid them entirely,
and use a special form @code{letrec} instead.  OCaml's @code{let rec ... and
...} is such a form: it looks like @code{let}, and the variables it binds are
bound simultaneously and have the same scope as each other.  The sample
procedure @code{f} above is written with it:

@example
let f x =
  let rec is_even n =
    if n = 0 then true else is_odd (n - 1)
  and is_odd n =
    if n = 0 then false else is_even (n - 1)
  in
  ⟨@var{rest of body of @code{f}}⟩
@end example

@code{Let rec} expressions, which have the form

@example
let rec ⟨@var{var₁}⟩ = ⟨@var{exp₁}⟩ and @r{…} and ⟨@var{varₙ}⟩ = ⟨@var{expₙ}⟩
in ⟨@var{body}⟩
@end example

@noindent
are a variation on @code{let} in which the expressions
@math{{⟨\kern0.1em exp_k⟩}} that provide the initial values for the
variables @math{{⟨\kern0.06em var_k⟩}} are evaluated in an environment
that includes all the @code{let rec} bindings.  This permits recursion in the
bindings, such as the mutual recursion of @code{is_even} and @code{is_odd} in
the example above, or the evaluation of 10 factorial with

@example
let rec fact n =
  if n = 1 then 1 else n * fact (n - 1)
in
fact 10
@end example

@enumerate a

@item
Implement @code{let rec} as a derived expression, by transforming a
@code{let rec} expression into a @code{let} expression as shown in the text
above or in @ref{Exercise 4.18}.  That is, the @code{let rec} variables should
be created with a @code{let} and then be assigned their values with
@code{:=}.

@item
Louis Reasoner is confused by all this fuss about internal definitions.  The
way he sees it, if you don't like to use @code{let rec} inside a procedure,
you can just use @code{let}.  Illustrate what is loose about his reasoning by
drawing an environment diagram that shows the environment in which the
@code{⟨}@var{rest of body of @code{f}}@code{⟩} is evaluated during evaluation
of the expression @code{f 5}, with @code{f} defined as in this exercise.  Draw
an environment diagram for the same evaluation, but with @code{let} in place
of @code{let rec} in the definition of @code{f}.

@end enumerate
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.21}Exercise 4.21:} Amazingly, Louis's intuition in
@ref{Exercise 4.20} is correct.  It is indeed possible to specify recursive
procedures without using @code{let rec}, although the method for accomplishing
this is much more subtle than Louis imagined.  The following program computes
10 factorial by applying a recursive factorial procedure:@footnote{This
example illustrates a programming trick for formulating recursive procedures
without using @code{let rec}.  The most general trick of this sort is the
@math{Y} @newterm{operator}, which can be used to give a ``pure
λ-calculus'' implementation of recursion.  (See @ref{Stoy 1977} for details on
the λ-calculus, and @ref{Gabriel 1988} for an exposition of the @math{Y}
operator.)  In a typed language, a procedure cannot be applied to itself
directly, because its type would have to contain itself.  The constructor
@code{Fix} wraps the procedure in a recursive data type, which OCaml
admits.}

@example
type 'a fix = Fix of ('a fix -> 'a)

let () =
  print_int
    ((fun n ->
       (fun fact -> match fact with Fix f -> f fact n)
         (Fix (fun ft k ->
            match ft with
            | Fix f ->
              if k = 1 then 1 else k * f ft (k - 1))))
       10)
@end example

@enumerate a

@item
Check (by evaluating the program) that this really does compute factorials.
Devise an analogous expression for computing Fibonacci numbers.

@item
Consider the following procedure, which includes mutually recursive internal
definitions:

@example
let f x =
  let rec is_even n =
    if n = 0 then true else is_odd (n - 1)
  and is_odd n =
    if n = 0 then false else is_even (n - 1)
  in
  is_even x
@end example

Fill in the missing expressions to complete an alternative definition of
@code{f}, which uses no @code{let rec}:

@example
type parity = P of (parity -> parity -> int -> bool)

let call p ev od n = match p with P g -> g ev od n

let f x =
  (fun is_even is_odd -> call is_even is_even is_odd x)
    (P (fun ev od n ->
       if n = 0 then true else call od ⟨??⟩ ⟨??⟩ ⟨??⟩))
    (P (fun ev od n ->
       if n = 0 then false else call ev ⟨??⟩ ⟨??⟩ ⟨??⟩))
@end example
@end enumerate
@end quotation

@node	4.1.7, 4.2, Chapter 4, 4.1
@subsection Separating Syntactic Analysis from Execution

The evaluator implemented above is simple, but it is very inefficient, because
the syntactic analysis of expressions is interleaved with their execution.
Thus if a program is executed many times, its syntax is analyzed many times.
Consider, for example, evaluating @code{factorial 4} using the following
definition of @code{factorial}:

@example ocaml
let rec factorial n =
  if n = 1 then 1 else factorial (n - 1) * n
@end example

@noindent
Each time @code{factorial} is called, the evaluator must determine that the
body is an @code{if} expression and extract the condition.  Only then can it
evaluate the condition and dispatch on its value.  Each time it evaluates the
expression @code{factorial (n - 1) * n}, or the subexpressions
@code{factorial (n - 1)} and @code{n - 1}, the evaluator must perform the case
analysis in @code{eval} to determine that the expression is an application,
and must extract its operator and operands.  This analysis is expensive.
Performing it repeatedly is wasteful.

We can transform the evaluator to be significantly more efficient by arranging
things so that syntactic analysis is performed only once.@footnote{This
technique is an integral part of the compilation process, which we shall
discuss in @ref{Chapter 5}.  Jonathan Rees wrote an interpreter like this in
about 1982 for the T project (@ref{Rees and Adams 1982}).  Marc
@ref{Feeley (1986)} (see also @ref{Feeley and Lapalme 1987}) independently
invented this technique in his master's thesis.}  We split @code{eval}, which
takes an expression and an environment, into two parts.  The procedure
@code{analyze} takes only the expression.  It performs the syntactic analysis
and returns a new procedure, the @newterm{execution procedure}, that
encapsulates the work to be done in executing the analyzed expression.  The
execution procedure takes an environment as its argument and completes the
evaluation.  This saves work because @code{analyze} will be called only once
on an expression, while the execution procedure may be called many times.  An
execution procedure has the type

@example ocaml
Value.env -> (Value.t, Eval_error.t) result
@end example

@noindent
and @code{Sec_4_1.analyze e env} analyzes @code{e} and applies the resulting
execution procedure to @code{env}.  @code{Sec_4_1.run_analyzed} runs a whole
program this way.

@noindent
The result of calling @code{analyze} is the execution procedure to be applied
to the environment.  The @code{analyze} procedure is the same case analysis as
performed by the original @code{eval} of @ref{4.1.1}, except that the
procedures to which we dispatch perform only analysis, not full evaluation.
Here are its first clauses:

@example ocaml
let rec analyze (e : Ast.expr) : Value.env -> outcome =
  match Ast.view e with
  | Ast.Scalar s -> fun _ -> Ok (scalar_value s)
  | Ast.Var name ->
    (fun env ->
      match Env.find env name with
      | Some v -> Ok v
      | None -> unbound name)
  @r{…}
@end example

@noindent
The simplest syntactic analysis handles self-evaluating expressions.  It
computes the value of the literal once and returns an execution procedure that
ignores its environment argument and just returns the value.  Looking up a
variable value must still be done in the execution phase, since this depends
upon knowing the environment.@footnote{There is, however, an important part of
the variable search that @emph{can} be done as part of the syntactic analysis.
As we will show in @ref{5.5.6}, one can determine the position in the
environment structure where the value of the variable will be found, thus
obviating the need to scan the environment for the entry that matches the
variable.}  The @code{Var} clause of @code{analyze} therefore returns an
execution procedure that looks the variable up.

@noindent
For @code{let} expressions, the right-hand sides and the body are analyzed at
analysis time.  The execution procedure evaluates the analyzed right-hand
sides and runs the analyzed body in the extended environment, just as
@code{eval_let} does.  An assignment also must defer storing the new value
until the execution, when the environment has been supplied.  However, the
fact that the assigned expression can be analyzed (recursively) during
analysis is a major gain in efficiency, because the assigned expression will
now be analyzed only once.

@example ocaml
  | Ast.Let (is_rec, bindings, body) ->
    let rhss =
      List.map (fun (b : Ast.binding) -> analyze b.rhs) bindings
    in
    let body = analyze body in
    fun env -> eval_let_analyzed env is_rec bindings rhss body
  @r{…}
  | Ast.Assign (reference, rhs) ->
    let reference = analyze reference in
    let rhs = analyze rhs in
    fun env ->
      let* reference = reference env in
      let* value = rhs env in
      (match Value.view reference with
       | Value.Ref cell ->
         cell := value;
         Ok Value.unit
       | _ -> type_error ":= target is not a reference")
@end example

@noindent
For @code{if} expressions, we extract and analyze the condition, consequent,
and alternative at analysis time.

@example ocaml
  | Ast.If (condition, consequent, alternative) ->
    let condition = analyze condition in
    let consequent = analyze consequent in
    let alternative = analyze alternative in
    fun env ->
      let* condition = condition env in
      let* b = as_bool "if" condition in
      if b then consequent env else alternative env
@end example

@noindent
Analyzing a @code{fun} expression also achieves a major gain in efficiency: We
analyze the body only once, even though procedures resulting from evaluation
of the @code{fun} may be applied many times.  A closure still carries the
syntax of its body, as in @ref{4.1.3}, so that the direct and analyzed
evaluators share one representation of procedures.  The analyzer keeps a
table from each body it has analyzed to its execution procedure, and the
@code{Fun} clause fills the table when the @code{fun} is analyzed:

@example ocaml
  | Ast.Fun (parameters, body) ->
    let (_ : Value.env -> outcome) = analysis_of body in
    fun env ->
      Ok (Value.closure ~name:None ~parameters ~body ~env)
  @r{…}
and analysis_of body =
  match Hashtbl.find_opt memo body with
  | Some proc -> proc
  | None ->
    let proc = analyze body in
    Hashtbl.replace memo body proc;
    proc
@end example

@noindent
Analysis of a sequence @code{e1; e2} analyzes both parts.  The execution
procedure runs the first and then the second.@footnote{See
@ref{Exercise 4.23} for some insight into the processing of sequences.}

@example ocaml
  | Ast.Sequence (first, second) ->
    let first = analyze first in
    let second = analyze second in
    fun env ->
      let* _ = first env in
      second env
@end example

@noindent
To analyze an application, we analyze the operator and operands and construct
an execution procedure that calls the operator execution procedure (to obtain
the actual procedure to be applied) and the operand execution procedures (to
obtain the actual arguments).  We then pass these to the @code{apply} of
@ref{4.1.1}, with a strategy that runs a closure body through its registered
execution procedure.  The body of a compound procedure has already been
analyzed, so there is no need to do further analysis.  Instead, we just call
the execution procedure for the body on the extended environment.

@example ocaml
  | Ast.Apply (fn, args) ->
    let fn = analyze fn in
    let args = List.map analyze args in
    fun env ->
      let* fn = fn env in
      let* args = eval_all env args in
      apply analyzed_strategy fn args
  @r{…}
and analyzed_strategy env body = analysis_of body env

and eval_all env procs =
  let rec go acc = function
    | [] -> Ok (List.rev acc)
    | proc :: rest ->
      let* v = proc env in
      go (v :: acc) rest
  in
  go [] procs
@end example

@noindent
Our new evaluator uses the same data structures, syntax procedures, and
run-time support procedures as in @ref{4.1.2}, @ref{4.1.3}, and
@ref{4.1.4}.  Both evaluators produce the same output for every program:

@example ocaml
Sec_4_1.transcript Sec_4_1.run_analyzed
  "let rec factorial n =
     if n = 1 then 1 else factorial (n - 1) * n
   let () = print_int (factorial 5)"
(* => "120" *)
@end example

